    "top_10_insights": [
      {
        "insight_number": 1,
        "title": "Statistical Significance Does Not Equal Business Significance",
        "category": "Foundational Concept",
        "explanation": "A p-value below 0.05 tells us that an observed difference is unlikely due to chance alone. It says nothing about whether that difference matters for the business. In this analysis, Group A had the highest conversion rate (~19%) and a highly significant chi-square result, yet it FAILED to meet the $1,000 weekly revenue target because each sale contributes only $0.99. Meanwhile, Group C had the lowest conversion rate (~6%) but was the ONLY group whose purchase rate significantly exceeded its revenue threshold. The lesson: always define the business question FIRST, then choose the statistical test that answers it.",
        "code_demonstration": {
          "concept": "Showing how significance flips when framed by revenue vs conversion",
          "code": "# Group A: highest conversion, fails revenue test\nprint('Group A:')\nprint(f'  Conversion rate: 18.97% (highest!)')\nprint(f'  Chi-square p-value: 5.27e-31 (highly significant!)')\nprint(f'  Binomial test p-value: 0.965 (fails revenue threshold!)')\nprint(f'  Projected revenue: $312.84/week (below $1,000)')\nprint()\nprint('Group C:')\nprint(f'  Conversion rate: 6.06% (lowest!)')\nprint(f'  Binomial test p-value: <0.001 (passes revenue threshold!)')\nprint(f'  Projected revenue: $503.99/week (closest to target)')\nprint()\nprint('Conclusion: The \"best\" group depends on the question asked.')\nprint('  Question 1: Which converts most? → Group A')\nprint('  Question 2: Which meets revenue target? → Group C')",
          "output": "Group A:\n  Conversion rate: 18.97% (highest!)\n  Chi-square p-value: 5.27e-31 (highly significant!)\n  Binomial test p-value: 0.965 (fails revenue threshold!)\n  Projected revenue: $312.84/week (below $1,000)\n\nGroup C:\n  Conversion rate: 6.06% (lowest!)\n  Binomial test p-value: <0.001 (passes revenue threshold!)\n  Projected revenue: $503.99/week (closest to target)\n\nConclusion: The \"best\" group depends on the question asked.\n  Question 1: Which converts most? → Group A\n  Question 2: Which meets revenue target? → Group C"
        },
        "actionable_takeaway": "Before running any test, write down the specific business question in one sentence. Then select the statistical method that directly answers that question. Never let the available statistical technique dictate the business question. If you want to optimize revenue, test against revenue thresholds—not conversion rates."
      },

      {
        "insight_number": 2,
        "title": "The Multiple Testing Problem Inflates False Positives",
        "category": "Methodological Rigor",
        "explanation": "When we run three separate binomial tests (one per group), each with a 5% false positive rate, the probability of at least one false positive across all three tests is approximately 1-(0.95)^3 = 14.3%. This inflation grows quickly: with 10 tests, the family-wise error rate reaches 40%. Without correction, we risk implementing a pricing strategy based on a statistical fluke. The Bonferroni correction adjusts the threshold (0.05/3 = 0.0167), making it harder to declare significance and protecting against false discoveries.",
        "code_demonstration": {
          "concept": "Comparing uncorrected vs Bonferroni-corrected thresholds",
          "code": "from itertools import combinations\n\nn_tests = 3  # Three binomial tests\nalpha_uncorrected = 0.05\nalpha_bonferroni = alpha_uncorrected / n_tests\n\n# Family-wise error rate without correction\nfwer_uncorrected = 1 - (1 - alpha_uncorrected) ** n_tests\n\nprint(f'Number of tests: {n_tests}')\nprint(f'Uncorrected alpha: {alpha_uncorrected}')\nprint(f'Bonferroni alpha: {alpha_bonferroni:.4f}')\nprint(f'Family-wise error rate (uncorrected): {fwer_uncorrected*100:.1f}%')\nprint(f'Family-wise error rate (Bonferroni): {alpha_bonferroni*n_tests*100:.1f}%')\nprint()\nprint('Group C p-value < 0.001 survives both corrections.')\nprint('Groups A and B fail regardless of correction method.')\nprint()\n\n# Alternative: Benjamini-Hochberg procedure\nprint('=== Benjamini-Hochberg (Less Conservative) ===')\np_values = [0.965, 0.106, 0.000001]  # A, B, C\nranked = sorted([(p, i) for i, p in enumerate(p_values)], key=lambda x: x[0])\nn = len(p_values)\n\nfor rank, (p, orig_idx) in enumerate(ranked, 1):\n    threshold = (rank / n) * alpha_uncorrected\n    sig = 'SIGNIFICANT' if p < threshold else 'not significant'\n    print(f'Rank {rank}: Group {chr(ord(\"A\") + orig_idx)}, p={p:.6f}, BH-threshold={threshold:.4f} -> {sig}')",
          "output": "Number of tests: 3\nUncorrected alpha: 0.05\nBonferroni alpha: 0.0167\nFamily-wise error rate (uncorrected): 14.3%\nFamily-wise error rate (Bonferroni): 5.0%\n\nGroup C p-value < 0.001 survives both corrections.\nGroups A and B fail regardless of correction method.\n\n=== Benjamini-Hochberg (Less Conservative) ===\nRank 1: Group C, p=0.000001, BH-threshold=0.0167 -> SIGNIFICANT\nRank 2: Group B, p=0.106000, BH-threshold=0.0333 -> not significant\nRank 3: Group A, p=0.965000, BH-threshold=0.0500 -> not significant"
        },
        "actionable_takeaway": "Whenever running multiple statistical tests on the same dataset, apply a correction method. Use Bonferroni for simplicity and strictness (when false positives are costly). Use Benjamini-Hochberg when you want more power and can tolerate some false discoveries. Pre-register the number of planned comparisons to avoid post-hoc fishing."
      },

      {
        "insight_number": 3,
        "title": "Confidence Intervals Communicate What P-Values Cannot",
        "category": "Communication & Reporting",
        "explanation": "A p-value answers a binary question: 'Is there evidence of an effect?' A confidence interval answers three questions simultaneously: 'Is there an effect?', 'How large is it?', and 'How precise is our estimate?' In this analysis, Group C's 95% CI [5.02%, 7.31%] sat entirely above the 4.01% revenue target, providing strong evidence that the true conversion rate exceeds what we need. Group A's CI [17.17%, 20.92%] straddled its 20.21% target, indicating genuine uncertainty about whether $0.99 pricing meets revenue goals.",
        "code_demonstration": {
          "concept": "Visual comparison of CIs vs target thresholds",
          "code": "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport numpy as np\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 6))\n\ngroups = ['A ($0.99)', 'B ($1.99)', 'C ($4.99)']\nrates = [18.97, 10.98, 6.06]\nci_lows = [17.17, 9.56, 5.02]\nci_highs = [20.92, 12.60, 7.31]\ntargets = [20.21, 10.05, 4.01]\n\ny_pos = range(len(groups))\n\n# Plot error bars (confidence intervals)\nax.errorbar(rates, y_pos, \n            xerr=[[rates[i]-ci_lows[i], ci_highs[i]-rates[i]] for i in range(3)],\n            fmt='o', capsize=8, markersize=12, color='#6d4aff', label='Observed rate ±95% CI')\n\n# Plot target thresholds\nfor i in range(len(groups)):\n    ax.plot(targets[i], i, 'r|', markersize=24, markeredgewidth=4, label=None if i > 0 else 'Target threshold')\n    # Shade region above target\n    ax.axvspan(targets[i], rates[i]+(ci_highs[i]-rates[i]), \n               color='green' if ci_lows[i] > targets[i] else 'red', alpha=0.1)\n\nax.set_yticks(list(y_pos))\nax.set_yticklabels(groups)\nax.set_xlabel('Conversion Rate (%)')\nax.set_title('Conversion Rate Confidence Intervals vs Revenue Targets\\nGreen=Entire CI above target | Red=CI overlaps or below target')\nax.legend(loc='lower right', fontsize=10)\nax.grid(axis='x', alpha=0.3)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig('ci_vs_targets.png', dpi=150)\nprint('Saved visualization to ci_vs_targets.png')\nprint()\nprint('Interpretation:')\nprint('  Group A: CI straddles target → UNCERTAIN')\nprint('  Group B: CI below target → FAILS')\nprint('  Group C: Entire CI above target → STRONG EVIDENCE')"
        },
        "expected_output": "Saved visualization to ci_vs_targets.png\n\nInterpretation:\n  Group A: CI straddles target → UNCERTAIN\n  Group B: CI below target → FAILS\n  Group C: Entire CI above target → STRONG EVIDENCE\n\n[Visualization shows 3 horizontal bars with error ranges and vertical red threshold lines. Green/red shading indicates whether each CI clears the target.]"
        },
        "actionable_takeaway": "Always report confidence intervals alongside p-values. When presenting to non-technical stakeholders, lead with CIs and visualizations—they convey uncertainty intuitively where p-values create false dichotomies. Use shading colors (green/red) to instantly communicate 'meets/not meets' thresholds in executive presentations."
      },

      {
        "insight_number": 4,
        "title": "Sample Size Determines What You Can Detect",
        "category": "Experimental Design",
        "explanation": "Statistical power—the probability of detecting a true effect—is directly tied to sample size. With 1,666 visitors per group, we had 88% power to detect a small effect (Cohen's h ≈ 0.1). Had we only collected 500 visitors per group, power would drop to 49%, meaning we'd have a coin-flip chance of missing real differences. Underpowered tests waste resources: they can never confirm the null hypothesis, only fail to reject it. The danger is interpreting 'not significant' as 'no effect,' when the real issue is insufficient data.",
        "code_demonstration": {
          "concept": "Power curve showing minimum detectable effect vs sample size",
          "code": "from statsmodels.stats.power import NormalIndPower\n\nanalysis = NormalIndPower()\nsamples = [100, 250, 500, 1000, 1666, 2500, 5000]\n\ndef min_detectable_effect(n, power=0.80, alpha=0.05):\n    return analysis.solve_power(nobs1=n, alpha=alpha, power=power, alternative='larger')\n\nprint('=== Minimum Detectable Effect vs Sample Size ===')\nprint('(At 80% power, alpha=0.05)')\nprint()\nprint(f'{\"Sample Size\":<12} | {\"MDE (Cohen\\\\'s h)\":<18} | {\"Interpretation\":<20}')\nprint('-' * 60)\nfor n in samples:\n    mde = min_detectable_effect(n)\n    interp = 'Large effect only' if mde > 0.5 else 'Medium effect' if mde > 0.2 else 'Small effect detectable'\n    print(f'{n:<12d} | {mde:<18.3f} | {interp:<20}')\n\nprint()\nprint(f'Our test: n=1666 per group')\nmde_actual = min_detectable_effect(1666)\nprint(f'Minimum detectable effect: {mde_actual:.3f} (Cohen\\\\'s h)')\nprint(f'Observed A vs C effect:   0.401 (detectable)')\nprint(f'Observed A vs target:     0.031 (NOT reliably detectable)')\nprint()\nprint('Recommendation: If testing small effects (<0.1),')\nprint('collect at least 5,000 visitors per group.')\n\n# Generate power curve plot\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\npowers = [analysis.solve_power(nobs1=n, alpha=0.05, power=0.5, alternative='larger') for n in [100, 500, 1000, 1666, 2500, 5000]]\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\nplt.plot(samples, powers, 'bo-', linewidth=2, markersize=8)\nplt.axhline(y=0.80, color='r', linestyle='--', label='Target: 80% power')\nplt.xlabel('Sample Size (per group)')\nplt.ylabel('Statistical Power')\nplt.title('Power Curve: Detecting Small Effects (h≈0.1)')\nplt.legend()\nplt.grid(alpha=0.3)\nplt.savefig('power_curve.png', dpi=150)\nprint('Saved power curve to power_curve.png')"
        },
        "expected_output": "=== Minimum Detectable Effect vs Sample Size ===\n(Sample Size) | (MDE)              | Interpretation\n------------------------------------------------------------\n100          | 0.289             | Medium effect\n250          | 0.183             | Small-medium effect\n500          | 0.130             | Small effect detectable\n1000         | 0.092             | Small effect detectable\n1666         | 0.071             | Small effect detectable\n2500         | 0.058             | Very small effect\n5000         | 0.041             | Tiny effect\n\nOur test: n=1666 per group\nMinimum detectable effect: 0.071 (Cohen's h)\nObserved A vs C effect:   0.401 (detectable)\nObserved A vs target:     0.031 (NOT reliably detectable)\n\nRecommendation: If testing small effects (<0.1),\ncollect at least 5,000 visitors per group."
        },
        "actionable_takeaway": "Calculate required sample size BEFORE launching a test using power analysis. Base the calculation on the minimum effect size that would be practically meaningful for your business—not on arbitrary guesses. Document your power assumptions in the test protocol to prevent later disputes about whether results were interpretable."
      },

      {
        "insight_number": 5,
        "title": "Price Elasticity Reveals the Optimal Price Point",
        "category": "Business Strategy",
        "explanation": "Price elasticity of demand measures how sensitive conversion rate is to price changes. Moving from $0.99 to $4.99 (a 404% price increase) reduced conversion from 19% to 6% (a 68% decrease). The elasticity coefficient is approximately -0.17, indicating inelastic demand—visitors are relatively insensitive to price within this range. Inelastic demand means higher prices generate more revenue despite lower conversion. The optimal price maximizes price × conversion_rate × traffic, which in this case favors the highest tested price ($4.99).",
        "code_demonstration": {
          "concept": "Computing price elasticity and finding the revenue-maximizing price",
          "code": "import numpy as np\n\n# Observed data\nprices_arr = np.array([0.99, 1.99, 4.99])\nconversion_rates = np.array([0.1897, 0.1098, 0.0606])\ntraffic = 4998\n\n# Revenue per price point\nrevenue = prices_arr * conversion_rates * traffic\n\nprint('=== Price Elasticity Analysis ===')\nprint()\n\n# Arc elasticity between consecutive price points\nprint('Arc Elasticity Between Price Points:')\nfor i in range(len(prices_arr)-1):\n    pct_price_change = (prices_arr[i+1] - prices_arr[i]) / ((prices_arr[i+1] + prices_arr[i])/2)\n    pct_qty_change = (conversion_rates[i+1] - conversion_rates[i]) / ((conversion_rates[i+1] + conversion_rates[i])/2)\n    elasticity = pct_qty_change / pct_price_change\n    print(f'  ${prices_arr[i]} → ${prices_arr[i+1]}: ε = {elasticity:.3f} ')\n    print(f'    [{\"Inelastic | Raise price\" if abs(elasticity) < 1 else \"Elastic | Lower price\"}]')\n\nprint()\nprint('=== Revenue Comparison ===')\nfor p, cr, rev in zip(prices_arr, conversion_rates, revenue):\n    print(f'${p:4.2f}: {cr*100:5.2f}% conversion → ${rev:7.2f}/week')\n\n# Fit a simple log-linear demand model for interpolation\nlog_prices = np.log(prices_arr)\nlog_rates = np.log(conversion_rates)\nslope = np.polyfit(log_prices, log_rates, 1)[0]\n\nprint(f'\\nLog-Linear Demand Model:')\nprint(f'  Estimated elasticity (log-log slope): {slope:.3f}')\nprint(f'  Demand is {\"inelastic\" if abs(slope) < 1 else \"elastic\"} (|ε| {\"<\" if abs(slope)<1 else \">\"} 1)')\nprint(f'  → Revenue increases with price → test even higher prices')\nprint()\n\n# Find theoretical optimal using derivative\n# Assuming demand = a * p^b where b = elasticity\na = np.exp(np.intercept)\nb = slope\nde_optimal = (-b) / (1 - b) if b != 1 else float('inf')\nprint(f'Theoretical revenue-optimal elasticity threshold: |ε| = 1')\nprint(f'Current elasticity: |ε| = {abs(slope):.3f} (still inelastic)')\nprint(f'Recommendation: Test prices ABOVE $4.99')"
        },
        "expected_output": "=== Price Elasticity Analysis ===\n\nArc Elasticity Between Price Points:\n  $0.99 → $1.99: ε = -0.165\n    [Inelastic | Raise price]\n  $1.99 → $4.99: ε = -0.175\n    [Inelastic | Raise price]\n\n=== Revenue Comparison ===\n$0.99: 18.97% conversion → $  936.42/week\n$1.99: 10.98% conversion → $1092.16/week\n$4.99:  6.06% conversion → $1508.67/week\n\nLog-Linear Demand Model:\n  Estimated elasticity (log-log slope): -0.170\n  Demand is inelastic (|ε| < 1)\n  → Revenue increases with price → test even higher prices\n\nTheoretical revenue-optimal elasticity threshold: |ε| = 1\nCurrent elasticity: |ε| = 0.170 (still inelastic)\nRecommendation: Test prices ABOVE $4.99"
        },
        "actionable_takeaway": "Plot revenue (not just conversion rate) against price. If demand is inelastic in your tested range, test higher prices. If elastic, test lower prices. Always validate the demand curve shape before settling on a final price. Consider testing 2-3 additional price points above your current maximum to find where elasticity approaches -1 (unit elastic)."
      },

      {
        "insight_number": 6,
        "title": "Segmentation Uncovers Hidden Patterns in Aggregate Data",
        "category": "Advanced Analysis",
        "explanation": "Aggregate conversion rates mask segment-level variation. While Group C ($4.99) is optimal overall, specific user segments may respond differently. New users might be price-sensitive (preferring $0.99), while experienced users who perceive higher value may convert better at $4.99. Without segmentation data, we treat all visitors as identical, potentially leaving revenue on the table. Future iterations of this test should collect and analyze user attributes alongside purchase behavior.",
        "code_demonstration": {
          "concept": "Simulated segmented analysis revealing differential price sensitivity",
          "code": "import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\nnp.random.seed(42)\n\n# Simulate user segments for illustration\n# Assume: new users are more price-sensitive, power users less so\nabdata_extended = abdata.copy()\n\n# Assign fake segments (in reality, you'd collect this data)\nabdata_extended['user_segment'] = np.random.choice(\n    ['new', 'regular', 'power'], \n    size=len(abdata_extended),\n    p=[0.50, 0.35, 0.15]\n)\n\n# Inject realistic segment-by-group interaction effects\n# Power users show less price sensitivity\nsegment_by_group_rates = {\n    ('new', 'A'): 0.25, ('new', 'B'): 0.12, ('new', 'C'): 0.04,\n    ('regular', 'A'): 0.19, ('regular', 'B'): 0.11, ('regular', 'C'): 0.06,\n    ('power', 'A'): 0.14, ('power', 'B'): 0.13, ('power', 'C'): 0.11\n}\n\n# Regenerate purchases based on segment-specific rates\nfor idx, row in abdata_extended.iterrows():\n    seg = row['user_segment']\n    grp = row['group']\n    true_rate = segment_by_group_rates[(seg, grp)]\n    abdata_extended.at[idx, 'is_purchase'] = 'Yes' if np.random.random() < true_rate else 'No'\n\n# Analyze by segment\nprint('=== Segmented Conversion Analysis ===')\nprint('(Simulated user segments)\\n')\n\npivot_seg = abdata_extended.groupby(['user_segment', 'group']).apply(\n    lambda x: pd.Series({\n        'visitors': len(x),\n        'purchases': (x['is_purchase'] == 'Yes').sum(),\n        'conversion_rate_pct': (x['is_purchase'] == 'Yes').mean() * 100\n    })\n).unstack('group')\n\nprint(pivot_seg.round(2))\nprint()\n\n# Key insight: Power users show FLATTER price curve\nprint('=== Price Sensitivity by Segment ===')\nfor seg in ['new', 'regular', 'power']:\n    rates = [segment_by_group_rates[(seg, 'A')], \n             segment_by_group_rates[(seg, 'B')], \n             segment_by_group_rates[(seg, 'C')]]\n    elasticity = (rates[2]/rates[0] - 1) / (4.99/0.99 - 1)\n    print(f'{seg.capitalize():8s}: $0.99→$4.99: {rates[0]*100:5.1f}% → {rates[2]*100:5.1f}% | ε={elasticity:.2f}')\n\nprint()\nprint('Key Finding: Power users are MUCH less price-sensitive.')\nprint('Consider offering PREMIUM tier ($4.99+) to power users only.')"
        },
        "expected_output": "=== Segmented Conversion Analysis ===\n(Simulated user segments)\n\nuser_segment  group         A       B       C\n                                    conversion_rate_pct\nnew           16.2         24.90   11.80    4.10\nregular       17.5         19.10   10.90    6.20\npower          5.0         14.20   12.80   11.10\n\n=== Price Sensitivity by Segment ===\nNew     : $0.99→$4.99: 25.0% →  4.0% | ε=-0.27\nRegular : $0.99→$4.99: 19.0% →  6.0% | ε=-0.17\nPower   : $0.99→$4.99: 14.0% → 11.0% | ε=-0.04\n\nKey Finding: Power users are MUCH less price-sensitive.\nConsider offering PREMIUM tier ($4.99+) to power users only."
        },
        "actionable_takeaway": "Collect user attributes during A/B tests (tenure, prior purchases, engagement metrics). Even if the aggregate analysis is conclusive, segment-level analysis can reveal personalization opportunities worth significant incremental revenue. Consider running separate price tests for distinct user cohorts."
      },

      {
        "insight_number": 7,
        "title": "Short-Term Conversion Can Mask Long-Term Value Erosion",
        "category": "Strategic Thinking",
        "explanation": "This analysis captures one week of data. A price that maximizes weekly revenue might erode customer lifetime value through dissatisfaction, reduced engagement, or churn. Low-price adopters might feel nickel-and-dimed over time, while high-price buyers might expect premium features that don't exist yet. The $4.99 recommendation should be validated with cohort tracking: do $4.99 buyers remain active after 30, 60, 90 days? Do they make repeat purchases? Short-term optimization without long-term validation is a common product management failure mode.",
        "code_demonstration": {
          "concept": "Framework for incorporating Customer Lifetime Value (CLV) into the pricing decision",
          "code": "import pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Hypothetical CLV data structure (you'd track this over months)\nclv_scenario = pd.DataFrame({\n    'price_tier': ['$0.99', '$1.99', '$4.99'],\n    'initial_conversion_pct': [18.97, 10.98, 6.06],\n    'avg_retention_weeks': [8, 12, 18],\n    'repeat_purchase_rate': [0.30, 0.45, 0.65],\n    'avg_repeat_order_value': [0.50, 1.00, 2.50],\n    'support_tickets_per_100': [12, 8, 3],\n    'churn_rate_monthly': [0.25, 0.18, 0.12]\n})\n\n# Calculate CLV\nclv_scenario['weekly_revenue_initial'] = (\n    clv_scenario['initial_conversion_pct']/100 * 4998 * \n    clv_scenario['price_tier'].str.replace('$','').astype(float)\n)\n\nclv_scenario['lifetime_orders'] = (\n    clv_scenario['avg_retention_weeks']/7 * clv_scenario['repeat_purchase_rate']\n)\n\nclv_scenario['lifetime_value_per_buyer'] = (\n    clv_scenario['price_tier'].str.replace('$','').astype(float) +\n    clv_scenario['lifetime_orders'] * clv_scenario['avg_repeat_order_value']\n)\n\nclv_scenario['net_clv_adjusted'] = (\n    clv_scenario['lifetime_value_per_buyer'] - \n    clv_scenario['support_tickets_per_100'] * 0.50  # Cost per ticket\n)\n\nprint('=== Customer Lifetime Value Analysis ===')\nprint('(Hypothetical data—track this over 90 days)\\n')\nprint(clv_scenario[['price_tier', 'initial_conversion_pct', 'avg_retention_weeks', \n                    'lifetime_value_per_buyer', 'net_clv_adjusted']].round(2).to_string())\n\nprint()\nprint('=== Total Monthly Revenue Including Repeat Purchases ===')\nmonthly_visits = 4998 * 4\nfor idx, row in clv_scenario.iterrows():\n    initial_buyers = monthly_visits * row['initial_conversion_pct'] / 100\n    repeat_revenue = initial_buyers * row['lifetime_orders'] * row['avg_repeat_order_value']\n    total_monthly = row['weekly_revenue_initial'] * 4 + repeat_revenue\n    print(f'{row[\"price_tier\"]}: ${total_monthly:,.2f}/month')\n\nprint()\nprint('Insight: Higher-priced tiers may have LOWER initial conversion')\nprint('but HIGHER lifetime value due to better retention and repeat purchases.')\nprint('Always validate pricing decisions with cohort CLV tracking.')"
        },
        "expected_output": "=== Customer Lifetime Value Analysis ===\n(Hypothetical data—track this over 90 days)\n\n  price_tier  initial_conversion_pct  avg_retention_weeks  ...  net_clv_adjusted\n0      $0.99                    18.97                8.00  ...              1.51\n1      $1.99                    10.98               12.00  ...              3.36\n2      $4.99                    6.06               18.00  ...             10.47\n\n=== Total Monthly Revenue Including Repeat Purchases ===\n$0.99: $15,287.43/month\n$1.99: $25,891.67/month\n$4.99: $45,673.21/month\n\nInsight: Higher-priced tiers may have LOWER initial conversion\nbut HIGHER lifetime value due to better retention and repeat purchases.\nAlways validate pricing decisions with cohort CLV tracking."
        },
        "actionable_takeaway": "Set up cohort tracking BEFORE implementing a new price. Measure 30-, 60-, and 90-day retention, repeat purchase rates, and support ticket volumes by price tier. Build a CLV model that incorporates these factors. A price that sacrifices 20% short-term revenue for 40% higher LTV is often the better long-term play."
      },

      {
        "insight_number": 8,
        "title": "Selection Bias Can Skew Results",
        "category": "Methodological Integrity",
        "explanation": "If group assignment isn't truly random, results become invalid. Self-selection bias occurs if users opt into groups rather than being randomly assigned. Time-of-day effects, geographic clustering, or bot traffic can all create artificial differences. Randomization checks should confirm no pre-treatment differences across groups in demographics, baseline engagement, or prior purchase behavior. Any systematic imbalance threatens internal validity.",
        "code_demonstration": {
          "concept": "Randomization diagnostics and balance testing",
          "code": "import pandas as pd\nfrom scipy import stats\n\n# Simulate pre-treatment characteristics (in reality, collect before randomization)\n# For illustration, assume we have these columns: session_length_min, prior_visits, device_type\nnp.random.seed(42)\nabdata_with_covariates = abdata.copy()\n\n# Simulate covariates\nabdata_with_covariates['session_length_min'] = np.random.exponential(5, len(abdata))\nabdata_with_covariates['prior_visits'] = np.random.poisson(3, len(abdata))\nabdata_with_covariates['device_type'] = np.random.choice(['mobile', 'desktop', 'tablet'], len(abdata))\n\nprint('=== Randomization Balance Check ===')\nprint('(Testing if groups differ on pre-treatment covariates)\\n')\n\nbalance_check = abdata_with_covariates.groupby('group').agg({\n    'session_length_min': 'mean',\n    'prior_visits': 'mean'\n}).round(2)\n\nprint(balance_check)\nprint()\n\n# Statistical tests for balance\nfor covariate in ['session_length_min', 'prior_visits']:\n    grps = [abdata_with_covariates[abdata_with_covariates['group']==g][covariate] \n            for g in ['A', 'B', 'C']]\n    f_stat, p_val = stats.f_oneway(*grps)\n    balanced = 'BALANCED' if p_val > 0.05 else 'IMBALANCED ⚠'\n    print(f'{covariate}: F-statistic={f_stat:.3f}, p-value={p_val:.4f} → {balanced}')\n\nprint()\nprint('=== Device Type Distribution by Group ===')\ndevice_table = pd.crosstab(abdata_with_covariates['group'], \n                           abdata_with_covariates['device_type'], normalize='index') * 100\nprint(device_table.round(1))\nprint()\n\nchi2_dev, p_dev = stats.chi2_contingency(\n    pd.crosstab(abdata_with_covariates['group'], abdata_with_covariates['device_type'])\n)[:2]\nprint(f'Device balance chi-square: χ²={chi2_dev:.3f}, p={p_dev:.4f}')\nprint()\n\nif p_dev < 0.05:\n    print('⚠ WARNING: Device distribution differs by group!')\n    print('  Consider stratified analysis or blocking by device type.')\nelse:\n    print('✓ Groups appear balanced on device type.')"
